{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#3190","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#3190","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#3190\",\"parent_revision\":\"6172b699b4e358db7cbc7f3360e4f02e0dd50fbe1f8d1bec7901492203e93b00\",\"root_revision\":\"6172b699b4e358db7cbc7f3360e4f02e0dd50fbe1f8d1bec7901492203e93b00\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P1\\ntype: Bug\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-23\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nWhen an item body contains the same `##` heading twice, the progressive-disclosure index reports each occurrence as a separate section with its own entry count, and navigating to either ordinal returns the entries of both.\\n\\nReported observation: a body with three real entries under a duplicated heading produces an index listing the section twice, each annotated `(1 entries)`, while ordinals 1 and 4 each return all three entries. Three entries exist; six are delivered; the counts shown are wrong in both directions.\\n\\n`plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tests/unit/test_content_normalizer.py:362-376` documents this behaviour as intentional.\\n\\n## Verification status\\n\\nReported by an analysis agent with the citations above. I have not reproduced it myself, unlike the sibling items filed alongside it (#3187, #3188, and the `strike_entry` section-filter item), where I verified the mechanism from source before filing. This item should be reproduced first during grooming rather than treated as established.\\n\\n## Impact if confirmed\\n\\nDuplicated content delivered as distinct entries inflates whatever a consuming agent counts or reasons over. An agent reading acceptance criteria would see each criterion twice and could treat the repetition as emphasis or as separate requirements. The entry counts in the index disagree with what navigation returns, so a caller cannot detect the duplication from the index alone.\\n\\nDuplicate headings are not exotic in this system \u2014 sections are written by several different agents during grooming, and nothing in the write path prevents two of them writing the same heading.\\n\\n## Not established\\n\\nWhether the documented intent in the unit test reflects a deliberate decision about duplicate headings or a test written to match observed behaviour. Reading the test and its history would settle which.\\n\\nWhether the write path can produce a duplicate heading in practice, or whether something upstream deduplicates before storage. If nothing can produce one, this is latent.\\n\\n## User-provided context\\n\\nNo fix direction is proposed, because the intended behaviour is genuinely unclear: merging duplicate headings, keeping them distinct with correct counts, and rejecting them at write time are all defensible, and the existing unit test asserts a fourth. Settle the intent before changing the behaviour.\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Session observation during messy-read risk analysis, 2026-08-23\\n- **Priority**: P1\\n- **Added**: 2026-08-23\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Impact If Confirmed\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nDuplicated content delivered as distinct entries inflates whatever a consuming agent counts or reasons over. An agent reading acceptance criteria would see each criterion twice and could treat the repetition as emphasis or as separate requirements. The entry counts in the index disagree with what navigation returns, so a caller cannot detect the duplication from the index alone.\\n\\nDuplicate headings are not exotic in this system \u2014 sections are written by several different agents during grooming, and nothing in the write path prevents two of them writing the same heading.\\n</div>\\n\\n## User Provided Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nNo fix direction is proposed, because the intended behaviour is genuinely unclear: merging duplicate headings, keeping them distinct with correct counts, and rejecting them at write time are all defensible, and the existing unit test asserts a fourth. Settle the intent before changing the behaviour.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Verification Status\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nReported by an analysis agent with the citations above. I have not reproduced it myself, unlike the sibling items filed alongside it (#3187, #3188, and the `strike_entry` section-filter item), where I verified the mechanism from source before filing. This item should be reproduced first during grooming rather than treated as established.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Not Established\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nWhether the documented intent in the unit test reflects a deliberate decision about duplicate headings or a test written to match observed behaviour. Reading the test and its history would settle which.\\n\\nWhether the write path can produce a duplicate heading in practice, or whether something upstream deduplicates before storage. If nothing can produce one, this is latent.\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"ff0d32e8ca1db4bf083d01dd301f7d4ffc398f34bd5c25afb8c397268858c975\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABQPoA2g\"}"}